A||||3||daved||Space frame repairs?||||1||
Z||000000||daved||04-04-2001||11:05 PM||||I bought that 84 T-top car in Toronto that JohnnyK was refering to in another post.  Unfortunately, the space frame is in very poor condition, particularly beside and beneath the battery, including the structural box section, and both fender/30Deg. box sections inside rear trunk.<BR>Has anyone affected repairs to these parts?<BR>Could it be done without welding? (many pop rivets?).<BR>Any idea what a body shop would charge to fix this sort of thing.<BR>I really love those T-tops, and damn-it, there's not a spot of rust on them!!<BR>Is the car scrap?  Any advice would be appreciated,  Dave||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||04-04-2001||11:11 PM||||As long as it isn't a structural member pop rivets may be used. Welding is recommended, at least by me, that is.It presents a much better appearance an is much easier to maintain and seal.<BR>Cost? / ? / ?||||reg||1
Z||000002||daved||04-05-2001||09:58 PM||||Thanks Indiana,<BR>Hoped to find someone who has done this repair.  <BR>I'm sure some members have come across cars with this problem, as I have seen two like this in the last couple of months.  Be a shame to scrap/part the car if it turned out it was easy and economical to fix!<BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||04-05-2001||10:17 PM||||I would second what indiana said.  I have done pop rivets before on floorboards, because there wasnt enough good metal to weld to.  But the floor board really isnt as much of a structural memeber as a rail is.<P>If you can get it welded or do it yourself, I would say that is probably the best you can do.  But make sure to get rid of ALL the rust before you do, otherwise the new pieces you weld it will just rust away.<P>Make sure to spray a few coats of rubberized undercoating on over your fixed areas to prevent further rust.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
